  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nA pattern change with cooler and drier air across the Northland mid-\nweek, then a return to warmer temperatures late week into next \nweekend. Occasional chances for precipitation, but no significant \nprecipitation amounts nor signal for severe storms through the next \n5-7 days, with long-range outlooks heavily favored toward above \nnormal temperatures through the end of July.\n\nLate today and tonight a secondary cold front moves north to south \nthrough the region bringing low level moisture along the front and \ncooler and drier air in its wake through mid-week as high pressure \nbuilds in with north-northwesterly flow aloft. The airmass aloft is \nso cool with 850mb temps of +2-6C it is actually below the tenth \npercentile of climatology for the International Falls RAOB \nclimatology for this time of year.\n\nThere are two areas of potential strong to severe storms this \nafternoon, though already as of noon the window of opportunity is \nlimited and closing fast for organized severe weather.\n\nNW Wisconsin Severe Threat: There is sufficient instability along a \ncold frontal boundary tracking east into northern Wisconsin with \nsteep mid-level lapse rates visualized on the water vapor channel \nsatellite imagery today. The biggest challenge will be whether there \ncan be enough forcing to kick off thunderstorms in our CWA before \nthe front exits to the southeast, and even if storms pop up in the 3-\n5 pm timeframe, the nearly 50 knot mean-level winds will quickly \ntake organized storms out of our CWA within an hour or two. While \nthe threat is conditional, the conditions should convection develop \nwould support all modes of severe weather with supercellular storm \nmodes, very large hail, damaging winds, and a tornado possible. \n\nOtherwise in northeast Minnesota: Behind the first cold front, this \nenvironment is not as extreme as northwest Wisconsin, but with 500 \nj/kg and 15-20 knots of deep layer shear a few strong to near severe \nstorms are possible. The early afternoon SPC Day 1 Convective \nOutlook aligns with our thinking of this being a Marginal (Level 1 \nout of 5) severe weather risk, with \"skinny\" cape profiles and dry \nair at mid/upper levels causing any initial updrafts to be fairly \nthin, though perhaps balanced with the limited deep layer shear. \nSmall hail/gusty winds likely the biggest impacts of the strongest \nstorms that pop up this afternoon.\n\nTonight the final cold front moves north to south bringing with it \nlow level moisture/clouds and then drier air in its wake. This will \nlead to cloud cover overnight into Tuesday with light rain showers \n(or perhaps even more like drizzle/sprinkles towards Tuesday \nmorning) but overall little in the way of measurable precipitation. \n\nWith the cooler airmass tomorrow highs will range from the mid 60s \nin the MN Arrowhead to the 70s elsewhere, with the Duluth forecast \nof 72 at the airport being the coolest day of July (coolest since 71 \non June 29) - and it'll even be 66 near the lake! Gradually warming \nup through mid week as an area of high pressure builds in. \n\nLate-week there will be a broad upper level ridge over the western \nCONUS and a deep-level trough over eastern Canada and the northeast \nleading to northwesterly flow aloft for the Upper Midwest. In this \nnorthwesterly flow there will be a few weak mid-level impulses with \nincreasing low level temps in the westerly flow at low levels. This \nwill lead to a few chances for precipitation late week into next \nweekend. Timing is tough to nail down due to the range in guidance, \nbut generally most scenarios look to be light/limited precip and no \nstrong signal for organized severe storms. Precipitation amounts at \nbest are likely less than quarter inch over the weekend (70-85% of \nensemble guidance). Highs back into the upper 80s and low 90s \nSaturday and Sunday, and Heat Advisories will likely needed for \nparts of the Northland.",
